   "properties": {
    "event": "Flood Warning",
    "headline": "Flood Warning issued November 19 at 1:53PM EST by NWS Melbourne FL",
    "areaDesc": "Brevard, FL",
    "severity": "Severe",
    "certainty": "Observed",
    "urgency": "Immediate",
    "effective": "2026-11-19T13:53:00-05:00",
    "onset": "2026-11-19T13:53:00-05:00",
    "expires": "2026-11-20T10:00:00-05:00",
    "ends": null,
    "messageType": "Update",
    "description": "...The Flood Warning continues for the following rivers in Florida...\n\nSt Johns River Near Cocoa 9w affecting Brevard County.\n\n.Extreme rainfall over the headwaters of the St. Johns River has\ncaused the river near Cocoa to rise through Major Flood Stage and has\nnow quickly passed record flood stage. Additional heavy rainfall over\nthe next 48 hours will continue to produce river rises above record\nflood levels through late week and into this weekend.\n\nFor the St. Johns River...including Cocoa 9w, Above Lake Harney,\nSanford, Deland, Astor...Major flooding is forecast.\n\n* WHAT...Major flooding is occurring and major flooding is forecast.\nThe forecast river level is above the all time flood of record.\n\n* WHERE...St Johns River near Cocoa 9w.\n\n* WHEN...Until further notice.\n\n* IMPACTS...At 17.2 feet, Water enters homes along the St Johns River\nin the Lake Poinsett area west of Cocoa, also low lying homes in\nthe Silver Pines subdivision in Rockledge and the Shakespeare Park\narea of Cocoa. Providence Road area west of Cocoa becomes\nimpassable, cutting off access to homes in the Mullet Lane and Bass\nRoad area. Docks flooded and water in parking lot at Lone Cabbage\nFish Camp.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 1:15 PM EST Thursday the stage was 18.3 feet.\n- Bankfull stage is 15.8 feet.\n- Recent Activity...The maximum river stage in the 24 hours ending\nat 1:15 PM EST Thursday was 18.3 feet.\n- Forecast...The river is expected to remain steady above flood\nstage at 18.3 feet.\n- Flood stage is 16.0 feet.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood",
